# Task Queue Sync (harness rule 13 — full detail)

Read when approving or closing a directive, or when a sync check WARNs.

**Opt-in.** This rule applies only while a tracker is configured below. To
turn it off for this project, set the kind to `none` and say so in
`log.md`. The issue is opened by the **Controller when the user approves**
a directive (not at proposal, so rejected proposals leave no issues), and
closed by the **Reviewer** at close-out. Title it `[<ID>] <directive
title>`.

`[SET AT SETUP: TRACKER_KIND]` selects which section below applies
(`none` | `gitlab-issues` | `github-issues` | other — add a new section
following the same shape if this project uses a different tracker).

<!-- SECTION:tracker_none:start -->
**`TRACKER_KIND=none`** — this project tracks work only in its directive
files (`.friday/active/harness/plans/directives/`); there is no external
issue tracker to sync. Directive headers say `Tracker: n/a`, and commit
bodies carry the literal `no tracker`. Skip the rest of this file.
<!-- SECTION:tracker_none:end -->
<!-- SECTION:tracker_gitlab:start -->
**`TRACKER_KIND=gitlab-issues`**

## What this rule is for

The files under `.friday/active/harness/plans/` are the harness's own queue. This project
also tracks work in **GitLab Issues** on the project `origin` remote
(`[SET AT SETUP: VCS_REMOTE_PROJECT_PATH]` on `[SET AT SETUP:
TRACKER_HOST]`), so that view must reflect real project state rather than
lag it. This is a hard MUST for both bookending roles (Controller at
approval, Reviewer at close-out).

## Credentials

`GITLAB_TOKEN` (a personal access token, `api` scope) lives in the
repo-root `.env` (gitignored, never committed). `GITLAB_PROJECT` in the
same file names the project path
(`[SET AT SETUP: VCS_REMOTE_PROJECT_PATH]`). Load both before any API call;
never print the token value in a report or log.

API base: `https://[SET AT SETUP: TRACKER_HOST]/api/v4/projects/<url-encoded
GITLAB_PROJECT>/issues`, authenticated with header `PRIVATE-TOKEN:
$GITLAB_TOKEN`.

## Controller — approving a directive

A directive is not approved until its GitLab issue exists. In the SAME pass
you set `Status: approved`, create the issue with a checklist mirroring the
directive's Steps and `Verify:` line:

```bash
curl -sS --request POST \
  --header "PRIVATE-TOKEN: $GITLAB_TOKEN" \
  --data-urlencode "title=<directive title>" \
  --data-urlencode "description=<directive summary + Verify checklist as markdown checkboxes>" \
  "https://[SET AT SETUP: TRACKER_HOST]/api/v4/projects/$(python3 -c "import urllib.parse,os;print(urllib.parse.quote(os.environ['GITLAB_PROJECT'],safe=''))")/issues"
```

Paste the returned issue URL into the directive header's `Tracker:` field.
This is a step of approving the directive, not an optional follow-up.

## Reviewer — closing a directive

A directive is not fully closed until its GitLab issue reflects that. Tick
checklist items as milestones land; in the SAME pass you set the directive
`Status: closed`, tick the remainder and close the issue:

```bash
curl -sS --request PUT \
  --header "PRIVATE-TOKEN: $GITLAB_TOKEN" \
  --data "state_event=close" \
  "https://[SET AT SETUP: TRACKER_HOST]/api/v4/projects/<...>/issues/<IID>"
```

**One directive, one issue.** If a directive is split or superseded, close
its issue with a comment naming the replacement directive(s), whose own
issues open when they are approved.

## Missing-credentials clause

**Block, do not skip.** If `GITLAB_TOKEN`/`GITLAB_PROJECT` are missing or
the API is unreachable, the Controller's approval step or the Reviewer's
close-out stops and surfaces the problem to the user rather than opening/closing the directive without its
GitLab mirror. There is no silent-skip path for this rule.

## Mechanical check (optional)

A script cross-referencing `.friday/active/harness/plans/directives/*.md` plus the
`closed` directives against live GitLab issue state, WARNing on (i) an
approved directive with no issue and (ii) a closed directive whose issue is
still open. The Controller runs it at session start and the Reviewer at
close-out. Its done-but-open output is a candidate list, not a verdict. A
WARN surviving two consecutive passes is reported to the Controller, which
raises it with the user.
<!-- SECTION:tracker_gitlab:end -->
<!-- SECTION:tracker_github:start -->
**`TRACKER_KIND=github-issues`**

This project tracks work in **GitHub Issues** on
`[SET AT SETUP: VCS_REMOTE_PROJECT_PATH]`. `GITHUB_TOKEN` (fine-grained,
Issues read/write) lives in the repo-root `.env` (gitignored); never print
it. The same bookends apply: the **Controller** opens the issue in the
same pass it sets `Status: approved` and pastes its URL into the
directive's `Tracker:` field; the **Reviewer** closes it in the close-out
pass.

```bash
# open (Controller, at approval)
curl -sS -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $GITHUB_TOKEN" \
  -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" \
  https://api.github.com/repos/[SET AT SETUP: VCS_REMOTE_PROJECT_PATH]/issues \
  -d '{"title":"[<ID>] <directive title>","body":"<summary + Steps/Verify checklist>"}'
# close (Reviewer, at close-out)
curl -sS -X PATCH -H "Authorization: Bearer $GITHUB_TOKEN" \
  -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" \
  https://api.github.com/repos/[SET AT SETUP: VCS_REMOTE_PROJECT_PATH]/issues/<number> \
  -d '{"state":"closed"}'
```

**Block, do not skip:** a missing token or unreachable API stops the
approval or close-out step and is surfaced to the user.
<!-- SECTION:tracker_github:end -->
